<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet component="quiz">
    <title>Learning Target 3,4,5 Quiz - MTH 204</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            This quiz covers course learning target #3, #4, and #5.
            Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
            Good luck!
        </p>

        <exercise>
            <!-- Learning Target 5 (Attempt 1) -->
            <title>Learning Target 5, First Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can reformulate and answer questions about span in terms
                    of linear combinations, matrix equations, and geometrically.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Consider the five vectors
                    <md>
                        \uvec = \threevec{3}{1}{0}, \,\,\,\, \vvec = \threevec{2}{1}{1}, \,\,\,\,
                    \wvec = \threevec{8}{2}{-2}, \,\,\,\, \yvec = \threevec{1}{1}{-1}, \,\,\,\,
                    \bvec = \threevec{1}{1}{1}.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                  <em> Note: In this problem, if you make a small entry error in
                    Sage, you will likely radically change the conclusions you
                    make.
                    Be certain to double check what you enter in Sage to make
                    sure it matches what you want it to represent.</em> 
                </p>

                <p>
                     <em>   You can and should use Sage for this problem. You can find a page of
                    Sage cells at <url href="gvsu.edu/s/0Ng"/> (zero, capital N, lower case g). </em>
                </p>

                <p>
                    For at least parts (a) and (b), be sure to use the phrase
                    "linear combination" in your response.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is <m>\bvec</m> in the span of
                        <m>\uvec, \vvec, \wvec,</m> and <m>\yvec</m>?
                        Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is <m>\yvec</m> in the span of <m>\uvec, \vvec,</m> and
                        <m>\wvec</m>?
                        Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is
                        <m>{\laspan{\uvec,\vvec,\wvec,\yvec} = \mathbb{R}^3?}</m>
                        Why or why not?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <!-- Learning Target 4 (Attempt 2) -->
        <exercise>
            <title>Learning Target 4, Second Attempt</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    I can represent a linear system in three different ways and
                    be able to translate back and forth among the forms: as
                    <m>{A\xvec=\bvec}</m>, as a question about linear
                    combinations, and as a question about an augmented matrix.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Consider the question, which you do not need to answer:
                    <md>
                        \textrm{\emph{Is}} \,\, \threevec{4}{2}{5} \,\, \textrm{\emph{a linear combination of}} \,\, \threevec{2}{0}{-2}, 
                   \threevec{1}{-1}{0}, \,\, \textrm{\emph{and}} \,\, \threevec{0}{-2}{2}?
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write an equivalent question about a linear system and
                        explain why your question is equivalent.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write an equivalent question about a matrix-vector
                        equation <m>{A\xvec = \bvec}</m> and explain why your
                        question is equivalent. In particular, include the matrix <m>{A}</m>, 
                        the vector <m>{\bvec}</m>, and how many entries are in <m>{\xvec}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <title>Learning Target 3, Third Attempt</title>

            <!-- Learning Target 3 (Attempt 3) -->
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    <em>   You can and should use Sage for this problem. You can find a page of
                    Sage cells at <url href="gvsu.edu/s/0Ng"/> (zero, capital N, lower case g). </em>
                    I can determine if and how a given vector can be written as
                    a linear combination of a given set of vectors and explain
                    what this means geometrically.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is the vector <m>{\bvec = \threevec{-14}{-38}{10}}</m> a
                        linear combination of
                        <m>{\uvec = \threevec{0}{-2}{2}}</m> and
                        <m>{\wvec =
                        \threevec{1}{1}{1}}</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Is the vector <m>{\bvec = \threevec{30}{10}{-23}}</m> a
                        linear combination of
                        <m>{\uvec = \threevec{0}{-2}{2}}</m> and
                        <m>{\wvec =
                        \threevec{1}{1}{1}}</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\uvec</m>, <m>\vvec</m>, and <m>\bvec</m> be
                        given by the figure below.
                       
                        Is <m>\bvec</m> a linear combination of <m>\uvec</m> and
                        <m>\vvec</m>?
                        If yes, explain why and find the weights; if not,
                        explain why no such weights exist.
                        <term>(Note</term>
                        <em> there is no scale on the axes, so you
                        can't determine coordinates for the various vectors;
                        just use the grid and figure appropriately.) </em>
                    </p>

                       <image source="images/LT3-grid21.png" width="45%"/>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>